WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.420
An axon grows toward the midline of the nervous system.

2
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.340
It crosses to the far side.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.560
Then it never crosses back.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.460
Its neighbours stay on their own side and never cross at all.

5
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.020
Here is the puzzle.

6
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.700
The midline did not change.

7
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.260
The same cells stand there before, during and after the crossing.

8
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.800
So what changed? The change has to be in the axon.

9
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:28.843
The midline holds a repellent called Slit.

10
00:00:29.603 --> 00:00:32.123
The axon carries a receptor for it, called Robo.

11
00:00:32.903 --> 00:00:36.003
When Slit binds Robo, the growth cone retreats.

12
00:00:36.643 --> 00:00:39.423
Axons that never cross carry Robo from the start.

13
00:00:40.143 --> 00:00:44.703
Axons that do cross carry high levels of Robo only after they have crossed.

14
00:00:44.923 --> 00:00:48.143
So the crossing axon is deaf to Slit until it is over.

15
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:51.047
The fly embryo exposed the logic.

16
00:00:51.627 --> 00:00:55.067
Its nerve cord looks like a ladder, with rungs where axons cross.

17
00:00:55.707 --> 00:00:59.267
Lose Robo, and too many axons cross, and some cross back.

18
00:00:59.787 --> 00:01:03.287
Lose a protein called Comm, and almost no axon crosses.

19
00:01:03.907 --> 00:01:06.947
Lose both, and the embryo looks like the Robo mutant.

20
00:01:07.507 --> 00:01:08.827
Comm works by lowering Robo.

21
00:01:09.227 --> 00:01:11.747
Once Robo is gone, losing Comm changes nothing.

22
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.770
Calm works inside the commissural neuron.

23
00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.850
It is made for a short time, before and during crossing.

24
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.730
It meets newly made Robo and diverts it from the road to the surface.

25
00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.510
Robo ends up in late endosomes and lysosomes, where it is destroyed.

26
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.790
Flies with unsortable Robo still develop normally.

27
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.810
The axon crosses with no Robo on its tip.

28
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.850
Then the neuron stops making calm.

29
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.310
Robo reaches the growth cone, and Slit now repels it.

30
00:01:39.033 --> 00:01:41.393
No clear vertebrate Comm has been found.

31
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:44.153
Mice use another protein, Robo3.

32
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:47.473
It is made on commissural axons before they cross.

33
00:01:48.093 --> 00:01:53.313
It blunts how Robo1 and Robo2 respond to Slit, while they stay in the membrane.

34
00:01:53.973 --> 00:01:58.633
After the axon crosses, the early form of Robo3 largely disappears.

35
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:02.073
Then Robo1 and Robo2 are free to respond.

36
00:02:02.813 --> 00:02:03.933
Comm destroys the receptor.

37
00:02:04.693 --> 00:02:06.293
Robo3 silences it.

38
00:02:06.983 --> 00:02:08.503
Both routes give the same result.

39
00:02:08.843 --> 00:02:12.743
The axon is deaf to Slit before crossing, and responsive after.

40
00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:15.403
So you can predict a midline phenotype.

41
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:17.123
Ask three questions.

42
00:02:17.763 --> 00:02:20.023
Is Slit there? Can the axon hear it?

43
00:02:20.403 --> 00:02:21.583
And when does it start hearing?

44
00:02:22.123 --> 00:02:24.963
An axon that hears Slit too early never crosses.

45
00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:27.183
One that never hears it crosses again.
